<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srirangam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Srirangam is a river island and temple town between the Kaveri and Kollidam rivers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is the site of the Ranganathaswamy Temple, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, one of the largest functioning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples in the world and the foremost shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Vijayanagara rulers enlarged it, and the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> worked there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srirangam.jpg" alt="Srirangam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IM3847 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Sri_Rangam_temple_near_Tiruchirapalli_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Kaveri divides at the Upper Anaicut, a dam at the island's western tip: the Kollidam, its first and largest distributary, flows to the north and the Kaveri itself to the south. At the eastern end the Grand Anaicut splits the Kaveri into four streams, one of which joins the Kollidam and closes off the island. The island is about 31 kilometres long and 2.4 kilometres wide, with the town at its centre. The name comes from arangam, the Tamil word for an isle among the branches of a river, with the honorific prefixes Sri and Tiru.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Old Tamil literature calls the place Tiruvarangam or Arangam, and the epic Cilappatikaram describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> reclining on the thousand-hooded serpent in the temple on the island in the Kaveri. Legend holds that Vibhishana, carrying Vishnu's image to Lanka, set it down on the riverbank, where it refused to move, and that the deity faces south towards Lanka to bless him. Tradition credits the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> kings Dharmavarcholan and Killivalavan, with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirumangai-alvar">Tirumangai Alvar</a>, with the temple's foundations and main buildings.</p><p>The theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> made his monastic home at the temple in the 11th century and wrote there his commentaries on the Brahma Sutra setting out Vishishtadvaita. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> treated the town on a par with Tirupati and gave the temple treasure, jewels and lands. The southern gateway tower, the Rajagopuram, was begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achyuta-deva-raya">Achyuta Deva Raya</a> and left unfinished for over 400 years, until it was completed and consecrated on 25 March 1987.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Srirangam is a neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>, and most of the island forms its Srirangam zone. The Ranganathaswamy Temple covers about 631,000 square metres within seven enclosures and has 21 gopurams. The town is the first of the 108 Divya Desams and home to many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavas</a>, and its economy rests largely on pilgrims, who come in the largest numbers for Vaikunta Ekadashi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Srirangam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Narasimha II intervened repeatedly to protect the weakened Chola king Rajaraja III against the Pandyas and the Kadava chief Kopperunchinga, and his son Vira Someshvara built a second capital at Kannanur Kuppam near <b>Srirangam</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was organised around temples, Brahmin assemblies, agricultural villages and merchant communities, and the kings lavished wealth on the great temples of Madurai, <b>Srirangam</b> and Chidambaram, where Saiva and Vaishnava devotion was central to public life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many poets sang of a particular image in a particular temple: Ranganatha at <b>Srirangam</b>, Vithoba at Pandharpur, Jagannath at Puri.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>By the account of a Sri Vaishnava temple chronicle, when Ulugh Khan, the future Muhammad bin Tughluq, sacked the temple of <b>Srirangam</b> in 1323, some 12,000 scholars and temple functionaries were killed; the teacher Pillai Lokacharya carried the processional image south, and Vedanta Desika hid the sacred manuscripts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
